            <h2>Airlines Flying from Miami to Lima</h2>
            <p>Three carriers operate nonstop service between Miami and Lima: American Airlines, LATAM Airlines, and low-cost Sky Airline, together offering roughly six daily departures.</p>
            <h3>American Airlines</h3>
            <p>Miami's hometown carrier dominates the route with its enormous Latin America hub, offering the most frequencies and the deepest onward connections.</p>
            <p><b>Highlights:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Up to three daily nonstops on Boeing and Airbus mainline aircraft with lie-flat business seats on select rotations</li>
                <li>AAdvantage mileage earning with Loyalty Points toward elite status</li>
                <li>Flagship Business on widebody rotations with premium dining and lounge access</li>
                <li>Admirals Club and Flagship Lounge access at MIA's Concourse D</li>
                <li>Single-ticket connections from across the United States through the airline's largest Latin gateway</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>LATAM Airlines</h3>
            <p>South America's largest airline group brings its Lima hub into play, making it the natural choice for travelers continuing deeper into Peru and the continent.</p>
            <p><b>Highlights:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>One to three daily nonstops on Boeing 767 and 787 aircraft with premium business cabins</li>
                <li>LATAM Pass earning with strong redemption value across South America</li>
                <li>Unmatched onward connectivity from Lima to Cusco, Arequipa, Iquitos, Santiago, and beyond</li>
                <li>Complimentary meal service and checked baggage on standard international fares</li>
                <li>Codeshare partnerships enabling coordinated schedules with US carriers</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Sky Airline (Low-Cost)</h3>
            <p>Chilean-Peruvian low-cost carrier Sky Airline has reshaped pricing on the route, frequently offering the cheapest nonstop fares between the two cities </p>
            <p><b>Features:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>One daily nonstop on modern Airbus A320neo-family aircraft</li>
                <li>Base fares that regularly undercut legacy carriers by 20 to 40 percent</li>
                <li>À la carte pricing pay only for the bags, seats, and meals you need</li>
                <li>A growing Lima hub with low-cost connections to Cusco, Arequipa, and regional South America</li>
                <li>Young fleet with slimline seating; bring your own entertainment and snacks</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Connecting Options</h3>
            <p>One-stop itineraries expand the field considerably. Copa Airlines routes through its efficient Panama City hub with multiple daily combinations. Avianca connects through Bogotá, Delta through Atlanta, United through Houston, and Mexican carriers through Mexico City . Connecting fares sometimes undercut nonstops by $50 to $150, and Copa's Panama hub in particular keeps total journey times competitive at eight to ten hours.</p>
            <h2>Seasonal Variations</h2>
            <h3>Peak Season: June through August, Mid-December through Early January</h3>
            <p>Two distinct peaks drive the calendar: North American summer, when demand for Machu Picchu and family travel surges, and the December holidays, when the Peruvian diaspora flies home.</p>
            <p><b>During peak months:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>July is the most expensive month, with average round trips climbing toward $700 and beyond</li>
                <li>December and early January fares spike as Miami's Peruvian community travels for the holidays</li>
                <li>Lima's coastal summer from December through March brings sunshine and packed beach districts</li>
                <li>June through August is dry season in the Andes - prime Machu Picchu trekking weather, despite Lima's gray mist</li>
                <li>Inti Raymi in Cusco each June draws festival crowds and tightens domestic flight inventory</li>
                <li>Book two to four months ahead for any peak window</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Shoulder Season: April through May, September through November</h3>
            <p>The strategic traveler's sweet spot, balancing weather, crowds, and price.</p>
            <p><b>Advantages:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Round trips commonly price 25 to 35 percent below July peaks</li>
                <li>September and October deliver improving Andean weather with thinner crowds at major sites</li>
                <li>Mistura food festival season and Lima's spring bring the city outdoors</li>
                <li>Better award availability and upgrade odds across all three nonstop carriers</li>
                <li>Hotel rates in Miraflores and Barranco soften noticeably</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Off-Peak Value Windows: Late January through March, August through October</h3>
            <p>Post-holiday and late-winter weeks produce the year's lowest fares.</p>
            <p><b>Benefits:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>August is historically the cheapest month for round trips, averaging near $414, with sale fares dipping lower</li>
                <li>January brings the cheapest one-way pricing of the year, averaging around $245</li>
                <li>Lima's coastal summer from January to March means sunshine exactly when fares fall</li>
            </ul>
            <p><b>Considerations:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>January through March is the rainy season in the Andes; the Inca Trail closes each February for maintenance</li>
                <li>Lima's winter mist from June to September keeps the coast cool and gray - pack a light jacket</li>
                <li>Holy Week (Semana Santa) creates a brief domestic travel surge</li>
            </ul>
